{"chunk_id": "fd8f75081543", "text": "The passage closes the cosmological discourse describing the sizes of celestial bodies such as the Sun and Moon and the role of Rahu in eclipses, asserting that these measurements come from scriptural science. It then shifts abruptly to the opening of the Bhagavat\u2011Gita Parva within the Mahabharata war narrative. Sanjaya arrives at Dhritarashtra\u2019s court and reports the devastating news that Bhishma\u2014the invincible grandsire and pillar of the Kaurava army\u2014has fallen, lying on a bed of arrows after being brought down through Sikhandin\u2019s presence in battle. Dhritarashtra responds with shock and grief, recounting Bhishma\u2019s unmatched valor and moral stature while questioning how such a warrior could be defeated. His lament underscores the tragic inevitability of fate and the catastrophic turning point for the Kaurava cause.\nSanjaya informs Dhritarashtra that Bhishma has fallen in battle, prompting the king\u2019s grief-stricken questioning about how the seemingly invincible warrior was slain.\nThemes: fall of a great warrior, grief and lamentation, fate and time, duty of Kshatriyas, battle narrative framing", "vector": [0.0007221201667562127, -0.0022689022589474916, -0.0012469444191083312, 0.012384641915559769, 0.0008670633542351425, 0.02003418281674385, -0.02481081895530224, -0.0037555438466370106, -0.024257006123661995, -0.01113856304436922, 0.025101570412516594, 0.014142997562885284, -0.0008657653816044331, 0.0172928087413311, -0.0015749054728075862, 0.015409844927489758, 0.048957064747810364, -0.007171877659857273, 0.018317362293601036, -0.02022801712155342, 0.006894970778375864, -0.009809411130845547, 0.010494754649698734, 0.014122230000793934, -0.028659818693995476, 0.024298543110489845, 0.026486104354262352, -0.028050625696778297, -0.006064251530915499, -0.012377719394862652, -0.008597945794463158, -0.00023169282940216362, -0.018054300919175148, -0.005922337062656879, -0.014842186123132706, 0.00215